%I A399464 #15 Nov 02 2026 16:42:35
%S A399464 1,1,2,3,5,9,16,28,53,98,180
%N A399464 Number of different ways n lines in the plane, no two parallel, can meet: the number of possible multisets formed by the numbers of lines passing through each intersection point.
%C A399464 For each point where k >= 3 of the lines meet, record k; two arrangements meet in the same way if they give the same multiset {k_1, ..., k_r}. Points on exactly two lines are not recorded: their number, binomial(n,2) - Sum_{i=1..r} binomial(k_i,2), is determined by the others.
%C A399464 The empty multiset (no three lines through a point) is counted, so a(1) = a(2) = 1. Arrangements of n lines in the real projective plane give the same numbers. Dually, a(n) is the number of distinct multisets of sizes of the lines with at least three points determined by n points in the plane.
%C A399464 Notation: 3^12 means twelve 3's.
%C A399464 a(n) <= A400149(n), the number of multisets that can occur in a linear space on n points (equivalently, the multisets for which the requirement "two lines meet in at most one point" can be satisfied); equality holds for n <= 6. Both are at most A072964(n-1), the number of multisets with Sum_{i=1..r} binomial(k_i,2) <= binomial(n,2).
%C A399464 The largest number of 3's in a multiset counted by a(n) is A003035(n), the maximal number of 3-point lines in the orchard problem.
%C A399464 335 <= a(12) <= 337: the multisets {4^5, 3^9} and {3^18} are undecided. The multisets {6, 4, 3^12} and {6, 3^14} can be realized by pseudolines but not by straight lines, so the corresponding sequence for pseudoline arrangements exceeds a(n) at n = 12.
%C A399464 Each realizable multiset is certified by an arrangement with coordinates in Q or Q(sqrt(d)), checked in exact arithmetic. Each multiset that is not realizable is excluded either by the inequalities of Melchior (1941) or of Csima and Sawyer (1993) on the number of ordinary points, or by an exhaustive search showing that no linear space with these line sizes exists or that none of them is orientable (admits an oriented matroid in the sense of Folkman and Lawrence).
%D A399464 S. A. Burr, B. Grünbaum and N. J. A. Sloane, The orchard problem, Geom. Dedicata 2 (1974), 397-424.
%D A399464 J. Csima and E. T. Sawyer, There exist 6n/13 ordinary points, Discrete Comput. Geom. 9 (1993), 187-202.
%D A399464 J. Folkman and J. Lawrence, Oriented matroids, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 25 (1978), 199-236.
%D A399464 B. Grünbaum, Arrangements and Spreads, CBMS Regional Conference Series in Mathematics 10, AMS, Providence, 1972.
%D A399464 E. Melchior, Über Vielseite der projektiven Ebene, Deutsche Math. 5 (1941), 461-475.
%H A399464 Petra Radko, <a href="/A399464/a399464.txt">Table of the a(n) multisets for n <= 11</a>
%H A399464 Petra Radko, <a href="https://gwynerva.github.io/line-pencils/sequences/realizable/">How n Lines Can Meet: realizable types</a> (with drawings of every type for n <= 11), 2026.
%H A399464 Petra Radko, <a href="https://github.com/Gwynerva/line-pencils">Programs, certificates, logs and verification notes</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%e A399464 For n = 5 the a(5) = 5 multisets are {} (general position, 10 intersection points), {3}, {3, 3}, {4} and {5}.
%e A399464 For n = 7 the multiset {3^7} is counted by A400149 but not here: it would be the Fano plane, in which every pair of lines meets at a point of three lines, and the Sylvester-Gallai theorem shows that this is impossible with straight lines. It is the first multiset where the two sequences differ.
%e A399464 For n = 10 the multiset {5, 3^10} is realized by a regular pentagram together with its five axes of symmetry. Every realization needs irrational coordinates; the certificate uses Q(sqrt(5)).
%Y A399464 Cf. A072964, A003035, A001200, A005834, A399605, A400149.
